A||||2||StuGood||Two-way check valve - what does it do?||||5||
Z||000000||StuGood||07-17-2000||02:50 PM||||There's a little rubber thingy that plugs into the end of the piston of the rear brake caliper.  The manual calls it a "2-way check valve."  Even the <B>name </B> doesn't make sense to me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> - all the check valves I'm familiar with are just one-way valves.<P>Does anyone know what it's for?  What is it supposed to do, and how does it do that?     ||||reg||5
Z||000001||PJ||07-18-2000||09:07 PM||||The rear calipers are a piston within a piston design. The inner one is for the parking brake and is actuated by a screw. The cutaway diagram in the factory manual shows an inner seal between the two pistons so there must be some independent movement between them. I would guess this "valve" lets a small amount of air in or out of the outer piston to equalize pressure. Eventually the inner seal begins to leak (you'll see fluid at the valve when you remove it or it deteriorates) and I know of no way to bebuild them. <P>I've found the car brakes better and more consistantly when I disabled the parking brake (at the caliper by cutting the threaded portion off the actuator).<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-19-2000||01:32 AM||||Well, bastante interesante!<P><B>Now</B> I know what was going on, when I saw traces of brake fluid under the inboard pad on one of my rear calipers.  Replaced it with a rebuilt, and everything worked fine -still couldn't help wonder what the valve did.           <P>Good explanation - now I can sleep at night.<BR>Thanks, PJ  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  ||||reg||10
